 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** $O(1)$ per face. It involves three vector additions, one division, and one normalization (square root and division) [G].
-- **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$ additional memory per face [G].
-- **Performance:** Shading increases both computing time and memory usage compared to flat shading [S1]. The cost is low per triangle but adds up over large meshes [G].
-- **Strengths:** Significantly reduces faceting artifacts on smooth surfaces or low-poly isosurfaces [S1]. It is computationally inexpensive compared to higher-order interpolation methods [G].
-- **Limitations:** Not necessarily appropriate in all situations [S1]. It does not account for curvature within the face, only the average orientation of the vertices [G].
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(F + V)$, where $F$ is the number of faces and $V$ is the number of vertices. One pass over faces for accumulation, one pass over vertices for normalization.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(V)$ for the accumulator structures.
+*   **Strengths:** Produces smooth shading on curved surfaces; simple to implement; standard technique for mesh rendering.
+*   **Limitations:** Does not account for surface curvature or area; sharp edges appear smooth unless hard edges are explicitly marked (not covered in sources); requires precomputed face normals.
+*   **When to use:** When smooth shading is desired for a mesh with known topology.
+*   **When not to use:** When memory is extremely constrained, or when flat shading is sufficient for the visual style [S1].
 
 ## Background
-This method is described in the context of the `rgl` package in R, specifically for rendering isosurfaces of density functions [S1]. The technique is used to smooth the visual appearance of multiple isosurfaces, such as those derived from tri-variate normal distributions [S1]. It is implemented by computing surface normal vectors at vertices as renormalized averages of the surface normal vectors of the triangles that share the vertex, which are then passed to the underlying rendering function `triangles3d` [S1].
+The technique is described in the context of R's `rgl` package for rendering isosurfaces, where it is used to reduce faceting artifacts by passing averaged normals to the underlying rendering function [S1]. It is also widely discussed in the context of Marching Cubes algorithms for generating meshes from voxel data, where it is essential for smooth visualization of the resulting surface [S2, unofficial]. The method is a standard practice in computer graphics for Gouraud shading.
 
